deep in the amazon jungle various  indigenous leaders have joined forces to  protect their livelihood we accompanied  native trackers during a week as they  police the javari valley reserve an area  twice the size of japan it's the home of  18 tribes 11 of them have had no contact  with the outside world  this part of the river belongs to brazil  where white men do as they please but  ahead of us is the indigenous javadi  territory it's forbidden to all but us  yet we're still in danger in the past  the threat came from oil companies and  loggers the government forced them out  two decades ago when the javadi reserve  was demarcated joel sampaio who used to  make a living cutting down trees in the  area is now helping the indigenous  tribes face a new enemy the increasing  number of poachers and fishermen who  have been sneaking into these remote  lands to steal their food  they've been illegally capturing  endangered and expensive species like  the piranha cool fish and turtles to  export them at high prices to  neighboring colombia and peru  outsiders are depleting this protected  forest of game the local indigenous  tribes hunt and fish just enough to  survive the illegal fishermen catch as  much as they can to sell abroad and make  money they have already had shootouts  with government authorities who tried to  stop them  the patrolmen are few in number but have  been armed by ngos with technology  satellite maps cellular apps and a radio  to communicate with villages inside the  javadi valley  we've been patrolling the takuai river  for two days now and the marubo  indigenous people have been showing us  how poachers and fishermen enter their  lands illegally they find these small  canals as you can see here behind me and  they ride these wooden canoes which are  very small and very hard to find  especially at night  we followed the intruders tracks into  the jungle our guide shows us how they  cleared a path to carry canoes to small  rivers further down which will take them  deep into the forbidden territory it's a  daily 10 kilometer hike through  unchartered lands with an occasional  stop to hunt for food on the way they  find new clues like this empty salt bag  the salt is used to preserve the fish  until they're taken to the market the  fish are taken to tabatinga five hours  down the river in the marketplace  smugglers also sell illegally hunted  alligators and turtles  but the real business is booming just  across the border in the colombian city  of leticia  all the fish here comes from brazil on a  good month this fishery sells 70 tons  the most precious species is the  piraroku one of the largest freshwater  fishes in the world matias pereira tells  us he caught almost 150 last week  i sell them in colombia because the  price here is better than brazil he  claims all the piraduku here are  certified and have come from designated  management ponds in the amazon like this  one but the colombian navy patrolling  the solymoise river says most fish are  illegally caught and smuggled  indigenous leader beto marubo says  illegal fishermen work for international  drug cartels as part of a money  laundering scheme  every little canoe can carry the  equivalent of eight thousand dollars  worth of fish and turtles and there are  many canoes working for one master we  set up our own patrols to prove to the  government what's going on and nobody  sees  he says that unless drastic measures are  taken many more endangered species will  continue to be smuggled out of the  indigenous territory threatening nature  and the livelihood of the tribes manika  yanakiv al-jazeera javadi valley
 

